Morning,

I need help with something that sounds simple but probably is not.

Analytics has been asked to report campaign success, but selected customers m=
ay already have been more likely to convert. There is no confirmed problem ye=
t; this is a request to test an assumption before it becomes policy.

The questions I would ask in the room are:
1. What would we expect to see if targeting explains the apparent lift?
2. What evidence would instead support that the campaign creates incremental =
response?
3. How do we rule out that service problems suppress otherwise suitable offer=
s?

This has returned because the previous answer was useful but not strong enoug=
h to become a standing rule. Use October 2024 as the new evidence point rathe=
r than recycling the earlier conclusion. Please work towards the treatment, c=
ontrol, success window and guardrail measures before the next campaign launch=
es. A useful output would be a one-page finding note plus the underlying CSV =
for our own review.

Use campaign design and customer responses; monthly account snapshots, status=
 events, limits, product enrolments and signatories; transaction-level timest=
amps, channels, amounts, statuses and error context; customer contacts, compl=
aints and suggestions. One caution: Historical campaign responses alone do no=
t identify causal impact where no comparable control group exists.

